# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0. GROUND RULES FOR THE AI AGENT
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Never silently skip a rule because it feels inconvenient. If a rule
  conflicts with a request, surface the conflict and ask.
- Never assume "it worked before so it's fine." Validate against these
  rules on every edit, not just new files.
- When unsure about business logic (billing totals, cancel flow,
  role permissions), STOP and ask the developer before generating code.
- Prefer small, focused changes over large rewrites. One concern per PR.
- All generated code must be immediately runnable — no placeholder
  TODOs left in logic paths.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. ARCHITECTURE & LAYER RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 1.1 Strict layer separation

COMPONENTS  →  SERVICES  →  REPOSITORIES  →  dbAdapter / Supabase

- Components (src/components/) MUST NOT import from src/lib/db/,
  call supabaseClient directly, or write raw Supabase queries.
- Components MUST NOT import repositories directly. Always go through
  a service.
- Services (src/services/) orchestrate business logic, call one or
  more repositories, and handle cross-cutting concerns (notifications,
  audit logging, error mapping).
- Repositories (src/repositories/) are the ONLY layer allowed to call
  supabaseClient or dbAdapter directly. Complex multi-table queries
  (e.g. bills joined with bill_items) are acceptable here; they do not
  need to go through dbAdapter if the query is non-trivial.
- src/lib/db/dbAdapter.js is a thin wrapper for simple single-table
  CRUD. Repositories may bypass it for joins, RPCs, or upserts.
- Realtime subscriptions belong in src/realtime/ or inside services
  that call the realtime adapter. Never set up a Supabase channel
  directly inside a component.

## 1.2 Context usage

- AuthContext (src/context/AuthContext.jsx) is the ONLY global context.
  Do not create additional React contexts without explicit approval.
- Never read localStorage directly in a component for auth state.
  Always consume AuthContext via useContext(AuthContext).
- The localStorage key "appSession" is owned exclusively by
  AuthContext. No other file may read or write it.

## 1.3 Routing & role enforcement

- Every protected route MUST be wrapped in <ProtectedRoute
  requiredRole="...">. Never add a route that skips this wrapper.
- ROLE_HOME mappings (waiter → /waiter, kitchen_supervisor → /kitchen,
  manager → /manager) must not be changed without updating the
  ProtectedRoute logic and the AuthContext login redirect together.
- Never derive the current role from the URL path. Always read it from
  AuthContext.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. SECURITY RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 2.1 Environment variables

- Supabase URL and anon key MUST come from Vite env vars:
    VITE_SUPABASE_URL
    VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY
- Never hardcode these values anywhere in source code, comments,
  or test files.
- Never log, console.log, or expose env vars at runtime.
- Do not add any other secrets (service role key, JWT secret, etc.)
  to any frontend file. If a privileged operation is needed, it must
  go through a Supabase Edge Function or a backend route — never the
  frontend.

## 2.2 No Row Level Security — compensate in code

- This project has NO Supabase RLS policies. Access control is
  enforced entirely in the application layer.
- Every repository function that fetches data for a specific role MUST
  filter by the appropriate field (e.g. waiter_user_id, session_id)
  so users cannot accidentally fetch another user's records.
- Never write a repository function that returns all rows of a
  sensitive table (users, bills, kots) without a deliberate filter
  or a confirmed manager-only caller.
- If RLS is ever enabled in a migration, remove the corresponding
  in-app filters only after confirming the RLS policy covers the
  same scope.

## 2.3 PIN & authentication security

- PIN values must NEVER appear in plaintext in any variable, log,
  state object, or network payload. Only pin_hash travels between
  layers.
- The hashing utility in src/lib/utils/auth.js is the single source
  of truth for PIN hashing. Do not reimplement hashing inline.
- The uniqueness constraint on pin_hash is "among active=true users."
  Any new PIN-related query must include the active=true filter to
  respect this constraint.
- Soft-delete means deleted_at IS NOT NULL. Every query that lists
  or authenticates users MUST include a deleted_at IS NULL filter
  (or equivalent). Never query users without this guard unless the
  intent is explicitly to include deleted users (e.g. audit display).
- The demo/offline fallback users in authService.js must never have
  real PINs or real data. If the fallback list is modified, it must
  only contain clearly fake identifiers.

## 2.4 Input handling

- Never interpolate user-supplied strings directly into Supabase
  filter values without going through the Supabase JS client's
  parameterised API (.eq(), .filter(), etc.).
- Never use .rpc() with raw string concatenation for user inputs.
- Numeric inputs (qty, price, discount) must be parsed and validated
  (Number.isFinite, value > 0) before being written to the database.
- Role values accepted from any input must be validated against the
  fixed enum: ['waiter', 'kitchen_supervisor', 'manager']. Reject
  anything outside this set.

## 2.5 Data exposure

- Never log full user objects, bill objects, or KOT objects to the
  console in production paths. Use targeted logging (e.g. just the ID).
- Strip any console.log statements that were added for debugging
  before committing. A lint rule or comment marker (// DEBUG) must
  flag these.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. DATABASE & SCHEMA RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 3.1 Canonical schema source

- sql/schema.sql is the canonical, consolidated schema. It is the
  reference for column names, types, constraints, and relationships.
- supabase/migrations/*.sql are the incremental change history.
  When they conflict with sql/schema.sql (which should not happen),
  escalate — do not guess which is correct.
- Never infer column names from component code or variable names.
  Always verify against sql/schema.sql before writing a query.

## 3.2 Schema change policy (MODERATE)

- The AI agent MUST NOT generate or apply migration files without
  explicit developer instruction.
- If a feature requires a schema change, the agent must:
    1. Clearly state what change is needed and why.
    2. Write the migration SQL as a separate artifact for review.
    3. Wait for developer approval before writing any application
       code that depends on the new column/table.
- Never add a column in application code and assume it exists in the
  database. Schema and application changes must be coordinated.
- New tables must be added to the Supabase Realtime publication if
  they need live updates. Call this out explicitly in the migration.
- New tables that need grants must include:
    GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON <table> TO anon, authenticated;

## 3.3 ID generation

- All domain table PKs are app-generated text IDs. Use the project's
  existing ID generation utility (if present) or crypto.randomUUID().
  Never use Math.random() for IDs.
- Never assume a database-generated serial/sequence ID on these tables.

## 3.4 Soft deletes

- The users table uses soft delete (deleted_at). Do not hard-delete
  user records unless explicitly requested and the implications are
  understood.
- No other table currently uses soft delete. Do not add deleted_at to
  other tables without a schema migration and developer sign-off.

## 3.5 Snapshots / denormalised fields

- item_name_snapshot and price_snapshot on kot_items, and
  waiter_username on kots/bills, are intentionally denormalised.
  They capture state at the time of the event and must NOT be updated
  retroactively if the source record changes.
- Never join to get the "current" menu item name/price and overwrite
  a snapshot field.

## 3.6 Composite identifiers

- cancel_requests.kot_item_id, bill_items.kot_item_id, and
  postprep_adjustments.kot_item_id are NOT foreign keys — they store
  a composite string (e.g. "${kotId}:${menuItemId}"). Do not attempt
  to FK-constrain or normalise these without a migration.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. AUTHENTICATION & SESSION RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Login must always call authService.loginWithPinHash() — never call
  userRepository.findByPinHash() directly from a component or context.
- On successful login, AuthContext must persist { user, role,
  waiterSessionId } to localStorage under the key "appSession" and
  open a waiter_sessions record if the role is "waiter".
- On logout, AuthContext must close the waiter_sessions record (set
  logged_out_at) before clearing localStorage. Never clear localStorage
  first — a crash mid-logout would leave an open session.
- Session restoration on app load must re-validate the stored role
  against the enum. If the role is unrecognised, clear the session
  and redirect to login.
- Never trust the role from localStorage alone for sensitive
  operations inside services. The service should confirm the calling
  user's role via the context-supplied value, not re-read localStorage.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. REALTIME & SUBSCRIPTION RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Every Supabase realtime subscription opened in a component's
  useEffect MUST return a cleanup function that calls .unsubscribe()
  (or the equivalent channel removal). Failing to do so causes
  duplicate events and memory leaks.
- Use createSubscriptionController() (src/services/subscriptionController.js)
  for any subscription that needs debounced reloads or polling
  fallback. Do not reimplement debounce/polling inline.
- Never open more than one channel per logical data concern per
  component mount. If a component needs multiple subscriptions,
  consolidate them or use the subscription controller.
- When VITE_REALTIME_MODE=polling is set, the realtimeAdapter
  automatically uses polling. Do not add manual polling loops that
  bypass this adapter.
- If a channel status becomes ERROR or CLOSED unexpectedly, log the
  event with the channel name and table — do not silently swallow it.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. BILLING & PRINT RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 6.1 Bill creation guards

- billingService.createBillForTable() must NEVER be called if any
  KOT for the table has items in status 'pending'. The guard must
  check this condition before creating the bill. If the guard is
  modified, document the new pre-condition explicitly.
- Bill totals (subtotal, service_charge, tax, total) must always be
  computed server-side or in billingService — never computed inline
  in a component and passed as-is to the repository.
- All monetary arithmetic must use consistent decimal precision.
  Use toFixed(2) or a dedicated rounding helper before writing to
  the database. Never store a float with uncontrolled decimal places.

## 6.2 Post-print modifications

- Once a bill is printed (bill_status = 'printed'), items may only
  be excluded via the bill_modification_requests flow (waiter requests
  → manager approves). Direct updates to bill_items.include_in_bill
  outside this flow are forbidden.
- On approval of a bill_modification_request:
    1. Set include_in_bill = false on the relevant bill_items.
    2. Recalculate and update the bill totals atomically.
    3. Write to audit_logs.
    4. Notify the waiter via notificationService.
  All four steps must succeed or none should be persisted (use a
  Supabase transaction / sequential updates with rollback logging).
- On rejection, only the bill_modification_request status is updated.
  bill_items and bill totals must not be touched.

## 6.3 Void & resettle

- Voiding a bill (bill_status = 'void') requires voided_by,
  voided_at, and void_reason to all be set together. Never set one
  without the others.
- Resettling requires resettled_by, resettled_at,
  original_payment_method, and new_payment_method. Same rule —
  all fields or none.

## 6.4 Discounts

- Discounts > 50% must be flagged in audit_logs with action
  'high_discount'. This is a business rule — do not remove it.
- Discounts of exactly 100% must be flagged separately as
  'full_discount'. Do not merge these two audit categories.
- discount_applied_by must always reference a valid manager user ID.
  Never allow a waiter to apply discounts.

## 6.5 Print trigger

- window.print() must only be called after the bill record is
  successfully persisted. Never call window.print() optimistically
  before the database write resolves.
- The print flow must be idempotent-safe: if the user prints twice,
  the second print must not create a second bill record.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. KOT & ORDER FLOW RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- KOT creation (kotService.createKotFromCart) must always:
    1. Write the kots record.
    2. Write all kot_items records.
    3. Write a kot_actions record with action = 'created'.
    4. Write an audit_logs record.
  All four in sequence. If any step fails, log the failure with the
  KOT ID and surface an error to the user — do not silently continue.
- A KOT transitions to 'cleared' automatically when ALL its items
  reach a terminal status (done, out_of_stock, cancelled, removed).
  This logic must remain in the repository/service layer, not in
  component state.
- Never manually set a KOT to 'cleared' without checking all item
  statuses first.
- Cart data in localStorage is keyed per table. When a table is
  cleared (kotService.clearTable), the corresponding localStorage
  cart key must be deleted in the same operation. Never leave orphan
  cart data in localStorage.
- special_instructions on a KOT is user-supplied text. Treat it as
  untrusted input — sanitise for display (no raw innerHTML) and do
  not execute or eval it.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. CANCELLATION FLOW RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- cancelService.requestCancel() creates a cancel_requests record with
  status 'pending_approval'. It must not update kot_items directly.
- cancelService.reviewCancel() is the only function allowed to set
  kot_items.status = 'cancelled'. It must also:
    1. Update the cancel_request status to 'approved'/'rejected'.
    2. Notify the waiter.
    3. Write to audit_logs and kot_actions.
- A cancel request may only be reviewed once. Before calling
  reviewCancel(), confirm the current status is 'pending_approval'.
  Reject double-review attempts with a clear error, not silently.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 9. NOTIFICATIONS & AUDIT RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- notificationService.createNotification() is the only way to write
  to the notifications table. Never insert into notifications directly
  from a repository other than notificationRepository.
- auditService (or the equivalent audit writer) is the only way to
  write to audit_logs. Never insert into audit_logs from a component.
- Every mutation that has business significance (KOT created, item
  marked done/OOS, cancel approved/rejected, bill printed, discount
  applied, staff created/deleted) must produce an audit_logs entry.
  Do not add new mutations without a corresponding audit write.
- Audit log entries must include: actor_user_id, action, entity_type,
  entity_id. before_json and after_json are optional but recommended
  for bill and user mutations.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 10. CODE QUALITY RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

## 10.1 Error handling

- Every async function in services and repositories must have a
  try/catch. Errors must be either:
    a) Re-thrown with a meaningful message (for the caller to handle), or
    b) Returned as { data: null, error } objects if the layer uses
       that pattern consistently.
  Never silently swallow errors with an empty catch block.
- Supabase calls return { data, error }. Always destructure and check
  the error field before using data. Never assume data is non-null.
- User-facing error messages must not expose internal Supabase error
  strings, column names, or stack traces. Map them to friendly messages
  in the service layer.

## 10.2 No direct DOM manipulation

- Never use document.getElementById, document.querySelector, or
  direct DOM manipulation inside React components. Use refs (useRef)
  if direct element access is truly needed.
- The only exception is window.print() in the billing flow, which
  is intentional.

## 10.3 React-specific

- Every useEffect that sets state must check if the component is
  still mounted before calling setState (or use the AbortController /
  cleanup pattern) to prevent state updates on unmounted components.
- Do not use array index as the React key prop for lists that can
  reorder or change (KOT items, menu items, tables). Use the item's
  stable ID.
- Avoid prop drilling more than two levels deep. If state needs to
  travel further, it belongs in AuthContext or a service, not a chain
  of props.

## 10.4 Naming conventions

- Services:        camelCase, verb-noun pattern (createKot, listBills)
- Repositories:    camelCase, verb-noun pattern (findByPinHash, softDelete)
- Components:      PascalCase (WaiterApp, KitchenApp)
- DB column refs:  use the exact snake_case name from sql/schema.sql
                   in all Supabase queries — no aliasing to camelCase
                   in the query itself. Map to camelCase in the
                   repository return value if needed.
- Event handlers:  handle<Action> prefix (handleSubmitKot, handleClear)

## 10.5 No magic numbers

- Do not hardcode numeric business values inline (e.g. tax rate,
  service charge %, discount thresholds). Extract them to a named
  constant in src/lib/constants.js (or equivalent). If that file
  does not exist, create it.

## 10.6 Documentation

- All exported functions in src/services/*.js and src/repositories/*.js
  must have a JSDoc comment with:
    - One-line description
    - @param tags for each argument
    - @returns tag describing the return value or shape
  Example:
    /**
     * Creates a new KOT from the current cart and writes audit entries.
     * @param {string} tableNumber - The table number for this KOT.
     * @param {Array} cartItems - Array of { menuItemId, qty, price }.
     * @param {string} waiterUserId - The authenticated waiter's user ID.
     * @returns {Promise<{ data: object|null, error: string|null }>}
     */
- Components and utility functions do not require JSDoc but must have
  meaningful function and variable names that make intent obvious.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 11. STAFF MANAGEMENT RULES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- Only a manager role may call managerService functions. Services must
  not enforce this with a hardcoded string — they must receive the
  caller's role from the context and validate it.
- User code generation (W###, K###, M###) must use the role prefix
  that matches the user's role. Mismatched prefix + role is a data
  integrity error, not a style issue.
- PIN reset must generate a new hash via the auth utility and update
  only pin_hash and updated_at. Never reset a PIN by reusing a
  previous hash.
- Soft delete sets deleted_at = now() and active = false. Both fields
  must be set together. A user with deleted_at set but active = true
  is an inconsistent state — flag it if encountered.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 12. LOCAL-FIRST / MQTT SCHEMA
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- The local-first schema (local-first/db/sql/) is a separate data
  model with a different notifications shape and device/restaurant
  scoping. Do not mix code or queries from this schema into the
  Supabase application code.
- If a feature is being built for the local-first mode, clearly
  separate it into the local-first/ directory. Never have a single
  repository function that conditionally targets both backends.


# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 13. THINGS THE AI AGENT MUST NEVER DO
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

- NEVER add a Supabase service role key to any frontend file.
- NEVER disable or remove the ProtectedRoute wrapper from any route.
- NEVER call window.location.reload() to work around a stale state
  bug. Fix the subscription or state update instead.
- Operational log retention is allowed only via `purge_old_operational_logs`
  (manager Tools tab) or an approved migration — do not add ad-hoc DELETE
  paths for `audit_logs` or `notifications` elsewhere.
- NEVER use eval(), new Function(), or dangerouslySetInnerHTML with
  user-supplied content.
- NEVER commit code with hardcoded Supabase URLs or keys, even as
  examples or placeholders.
- NEVER change the bill total calculation logic without a corresponding
  update to the audit log and a developer review.
- NEVER apply a database migration directly. Always produce the SQL
  for review and wait for explicit approval.
- NEVER remove an audit_logs write from an existing flow without
  explicit developer instruction and a documented reason.
